### Request
POST /api/v1/hello HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:19320

### Response (captured 2026-07-31)
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
Content-Length: 21
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 13:58:24 GMT
Server: Kestrel

{"error":"not found"}

### Additional captures (same behavior observed)
PUT /api/v1/hello -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
DELETE /api/v1/hello -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
OPTIONS /api/v1/hello -> 404 {"error":"not found"}
HEAD /api/v1/hello -> 404 (no body, per HEAD semantics)
POST /api/v1/health -> 404 {"error":"not found"}

### Observation
Sending any non-GET method to a declared route falls through Giraffe's `choose` combinator
straight to the catch-all `notFoundHandler`, returning the same `{"error":"not found"}` 404
envelope as a genuinely unknown path. This matches the OpenAPI `info.description`'s own framing
("Exactly three GET routes ... and a 404 handler for anything else") -- it is intended Phase-1
behavior, not a defect. No existing Gherkin scenario exercises a method mismatch on a *valid*
path though; only `greeting.feature`'s "An unknown route is refused" scenario, which targets an
entirely nonexistent path (`/api/v1/does-not-exist`). Proposed as SG-003.
